  "documentation": {
    "range": "In the south-east Atlantic, this species is present from Morocco to the English Channel. It is present in the west and north-east part of the Mediterranean Sea and in the west part of the Black Sea.<br/>It is typically found from 10 to 100 m in the Atlantic and from 30 to 100 m in the Mediterranean (Zander 1986).",
    "population": "Collections for this species are common in museums with typically less than ten specimens per collection. It was collected in about 37% of experimental trawls conducted in the Aegean Sea, but was not abundant (Damalas&#160;et al.&#160;2010).",
    "habitats": "This species lives on muddy bottoms. It was found in a <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">Halophila stipulacea</span> bed in a coastal zone of Sicily (Di Martino et al. 2007). This species is mainly nocturnal and feeds on small invertebrates. It spawns in April (Marseille) or July (England); eggs are laid under mussel-shells or stones and guarded by the male (Zander 1986).",
    "threats": "This species is often caught by trawlers, but is discarded. There are no known major threats.",
    "measures": "No specific conservation measures are in place for this species. However, it may be found in Marine Protected Areas within its range.&#160;<em>Blennius ocellaris</em>&#160;is often caught by trawlers, but is discarded. It may be impacted locally by dredging in parts of the Mediterranean.",
    "use_trade": null,
    "rationale": "<strong>Global Assessment</strong>: This species is widespread from the English Channel to Morocco and throughout the Mediterranean Sea and parts of the Red Sea. There are no major threats to <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">Blennius ocellaris </span>although it may sometimes be captured as bycatch in bottom trawlers. This species is globally listed as Least Concern.<br/><br/><strong>European Regional Assessment:&#160;</strong><br/><em>Blennius ocellaris</em>&#160;is widespread from the English Channel to Morocco, including the Mediterranean Sea and parts of the Black Sea. Though little is known about the population status or trends, it is common throughout parts of its range. There are no major threats to the species. Therefore,&#160;<em>Blennius ocellaris</em>&#160;is regionally assessed as Least Concern in Europe.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
